<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malbork Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marienburg, Castle of the Teutonic Order in Malbork</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a> Castle, German Marienburg, is a vast brick fortress on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> river in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> from the late 13th century. From 1309 it was the seat of the Grand Master and capital of the Order's state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. It withstood a Polish siege after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> in 1410, but passed to Poland in 1457 and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malbork-castle.jpg" alt="Malbork Castle" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gregy · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zesp%C3%B3%C5%82_Zamku_Krzy%C5%BCackiego_MALBORK_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
